Connection notes:
- The link was intermittent at first: the board would enumerate then drop. Root
  cause was a marginal USB-C connection (originally through a hub / Type-C AV
  adapter; flaky physical contact). Removing the hub and reseating a data cable
  produced a usable port. A background watcher caught /dev/cu.usbmodem1101.
- The ESP32-S3 native USB enumerates as "USB JTAG/serial debug unit"; when macOS
  shows it !matched with no /dev node, reseat/retry until a usbmodem node binds.
  No driver install was needed once the data link was solid.

Device identification (read by esptool v4.11.0, DEVICE VERIFIED):
- Chip: ESP32-S3-PICO-1 (LGA56), revision v0.2
- Features: WiFi, BLE, Embedded Flash 8MB (GigaDevice), Embedded PSRAM 8MB (AP_3v3)
- USB mode: USB-Serial/JTAG (baud setting ignored; runs at USB speed)
- MAC: ac:27:6e:d2:07:28
- Flash: manufacturer c8 (GigaDevice), device 4017, 8 MB; eFuse quad (QIO), 3.3 V
- Crystal: 40 MHz
=> This unit is a V2 board (8 MB flash / 8 MB PSRAM). V1-vs-V2 question RESOLVED.
   Touch-vs-non-touch and battery-vs-EN are not determinable from the flash read
   and still need visual confirmation.

Commands:
- Detect: python3 -m esptool --port /dev/cu.usbmodem1101 flash_id
- Backup: python3 -m esptool --port /dev/cu.usbmodem1101 --baud 921600 \
    read_flash 0 0x800000 esp32s3-epaper154-full-flash-2026-06-29.bin

